Architecture:
- 40image.c: Main driver, handles command-line arguments and calls compression 
    or decompression functions
- compress40.c: Implements compression, converting RGB to YPbPr, applying DCT,
    packing values, and writing binary output
- compression.c/h: Handles conversion from RGB to YPbPr, DCT transformation, 
    quantization, and bit-packing
- decompression.c/h: Handles bit-unpacking, inverse DCT, and YPbPr to RGB 
    conversion
- bitpack.c: Implements bit-packing utilities for extracting and setting 
    values in a 64-bit word

- Implemented image compression and decompression, ensuring 
that images are compressed using discrete cosine transformation (DCT) and 
bit-packed into 64bit words. The compression process effectively reduces the 
image size while maintaining visual accuracy. The program correctly converts 
images from RGB to the YPbPr color space, applies dct, quantizes values, and 
packs them into binary codewords. The decompression process successfully 
reverses these steps to reconstruct the original image.
- Encountered an issue where the decompressed images contained unexpected 
blue and red dots. After debugging, determined that this was due to 
incorrect clamp values when converting Y, Pb, and Pr back to RGB. Adjusting 
these clamp values ensured that color values remained within their valid 
range, resolving the issue. Additionally, verified that the bit-packing and
unpacking functions correctly handle integer overflow and boundary cases.
The program was tested against PPM images and successfully produced compressed
outputs that matched the expected size reductions.

Time Spent:
- 30 hours analyzing the problem
- 40 hours implementing and testing
- 70 hours total